        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How do you get on a treadmill safely?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Stand with a foot on each side rail, holding the handrails, and clip the safety key to your waistband. Start the belt at its lowest speed, then step on one foot at a time while still holding on, walk in the centre of the belt and increase the speed gradually.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do you step onto a moving treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Ideally you do not: start the belt with your feet on the side rails instead. If you must, slow the belt to a slow walk first, straddle the side rails holding both handrails, let one foot move with the belt for a few strides, then bring the other on. Do not step onto a fast belt or step on from behind, and older users or anyone with balance problems should stop the belt and restart it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should you wait for the treadmill to stop before getting off?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Lower the incline to zero, slow to the lowest speed, press stop and keep walking until the belt has completely stopped, then step off to the side holding a rail. Stepping off a moving belt is one of the most common ways people fall.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where should the treadmill safety key be clipped?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              To your clothing at the waist — a firm waistband, belt or belt loop at the front or side. Clipped to loose fabric it may tear rather than pull the key out. Test it occasionally at the slowest speed, holding the rails, to confirm the belt stops.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space do you need behind a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Follow your manual. ICON-built machines such as NordicTrack and ProForm commonly ask for at least 8 feet behind and 2 feet each side; a Life Fitness manual summarising ASTM F2115 gives 2 metres behind and 0.5 metres each side. Treat about 2 metres, or 6.5 feet, as a minimum and keep it permanently clear.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill safe for elderly people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For many older adults, yes, with the right routine and machine: long handrails, a low step-up height, a slow starting speed and the safety key clipped on every time. Anyone with a heart condition, history of falls, dizziness or a balance disorder should ask a doctor or physical therapist first, and have someone nearby for the first sessions.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What should you do if you stumble on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Grab the handrails, put your feet out onto the side rails so you are off the belt, then stop the machine. If you fall, do not try to stand up on a moving belt; the clipped-on key should stop it. Roll off to the side if it does not, and check for injury before getting up slowly.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

